  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nHigh pressure will remain in control through the weekend and into \nearly next week,  maintaining hot and mostly dry conditions across \nSouth Texas. Isolated showers and thunderstorms will remain \npossible each afternoon along the sea breeze, through measurable \nrainfall will remain limited for most locations.\n\nAttention then turns to the middle and latter portions of next week \nas the upper-level pattern begins to change. A mid-level low is \nforecast to move across the Desert Southwest while remnants of an \neastern Pacific tropical system advances across northern Mexico. \nIncreasing moisture associated with these features should support an \nincrease in rain and thunderstorm chances beginning around midweek.\nA cold front may also approach South Texas during this time, \nalthough considerable uncertainty remains regarding how far south \nthe boundary will progress before potentially stalling north of the \nCWA. Given the uncertainty in the evolution and timing of these \nfeatures, will continue to lean toward the NBM for now, with low to \nmoderate rain chances beginning around midweek. \n\nAbove-normal temperatures will continue through early next week \nbefore trending slightly cooler during the latter half of the week \nas rain chances increase and the overall pattern changes. Afternoon \nheat index values will generally range between 100-105 degrees, with \nlocalized values approaching 110 at times. Overnight lows remain \nquite warm in the mid to upper 70's. Some relief from the heat may \narrive during the latter half of next week, through the magnitude of \nany cooling will depend on the evolution of the approaching front \nand increasing rain chances.",
